what is a coding assistent?

Coding assistants use language models to complete different tasks
Language models need tools to handle most real-world programming tasks
Not all language models use tools with the same skill level
Claude's strong tool use enables better security, customization, and longevity in Claude Code
This tool-use capability is what transforms a simple text-generating model into a powerful coding assistant that can read your files, understand your codebase, and make meaningful changes to your projects.




The Big Idea

AI coding assistants have a limited context window. If you give them too much unrelated code, their performance drops. The goal is to provide:

✅ Relevant files
✅ Important architecture information
✅ Project-specific instructions

while avoiding unnecessary context.

1. /init Command

When you start working on a new project, run:

/init
What it does

Claude scans your codebase and learns:

What the project does
Project architecture
Important files
Build/test commands
Coding conventions
Common patterns
Result

Claude creates a file:

CLAUDE.md

This file becomes a permanent guide for future conversations.

Think of /init as:

"Create a manual so you don't have to explain my project every time."

2. What is CLAUDE.md?

CLAUDE.md is essentially a project memory file.

Every time Claude receives a request, it automatically reads this file first.

It contains:
Project information

Example:

# Project Overview

This is a Next.js SaaS application.

## Commands

npm run dev
npm run test

## Architecture

Frontend: Next.js
Backend: Express
Database: PostgreSQL
Custom instructions

Example:

Use comments sparingly.
Prefer functional React components.
Avoid creating unnecessary abstractions.

This makes Claude behave consistently across sessions.

3. Three Types of CLAUDE Memory Files
A. Project Memory
CLAUDE.md

Stored in project root.

Purpose:

Shared with team
Committed to Git
Documents project-wide instructions

Example:

my-app/
 ├─ CLAUDE.md
 ├─ src/
 └─ package.json
B. Personal Project Memory
CLAUDE.local.md

Purpose:

Only for you
Not committed to Git
Personal preferences

Example:

Explain complex changes before editing.
Always suggest tests.

Your teammates won't see these instructions.

C. Global Memory
~/.claude/CLAUDE.md

Purpose:

Applies to every project
Machine-wide preferences

Example:

Prefer TypeScript.
Use concise explanations.
Avoid excessive comments.

Every project inherits these instructions.

4. Updating Claude's Behavior

If Claude repeatedly does something you dislike, update the memory.

Example:

Claude writes too many comments.

Add:

Use comments sparingly.
Only comment complex code.

Now Claude will remember this in future chats.

Instead of repeatedly saying:

"Don't add so many comments"

you teach it once.

5. /memory Command

You can edit memory files directly.

Run:

/memory

Claude opens the memory file for editing.

After saving changes:

New instructions apply immediately
Future conversations follow them

Think of it as:

"Editing Claude's operating manual."

6. Using @ to Include Files

Sometimes Claude only needs one or two files.

Use:

@filename

Example:

How does authentication work?

@auth

Claude will:

Find matching auth files
Let you choose
Load them into context

This is better than:

Please search my entire codebase...

because it gives Claude only relevant information.

7. Referencing Files Inside CLAUDE.md

You can permanently attach important files.

Example:

The database schema is defined in:

@prisma/schema.prisma

Reference it whenever you need to understand the data model.

Now every request automatically includes the schema.

Benefits:

Claude instantly understands tables
Knows relationships
Understands data structure

without searching every time.

8. Importing Existing Documentation

Suppose your repo already contains:

AGENTS.md

Instead of copying instructions into CLAUDE.md:

@AGENTS.md

Additional Claude-specific instructions here...

Claude loads:

AGENTS.md
Then your Claude-specific rules

This avoids duplicate documentation.

Practical Workflow

A good workflow looks like this:

Step 1

Run:

/init

Generate project memory.

Step 2

Edit CLAUDE.md

Add:

Prefer TypeScript.
Use comments sparingly.
Run tests after changes.
Step 3

Reference important files:

@prisma/schema.prisma
@docs/architecture.md
Step 4

When asking questions, include relevant files:

Why is login failing?

@src/auth/login.ts
@src/auth/session.ts
Step 5

Let Claude work with focused context instead of the entire repository.

Mental Model

Think of Claude's context like a backpack.

Too little information → Claude gets confused.
Too much information → Claude gets overloaded.
The right information → Claude performs best.

The tools help manage that balance:

Feature	Purpose
/init	Creates project memory
CLAUDE.md	Shared project instructions
CLAUDE.local.md	Personal instructions
~/.claude/CLAUDE.md	Global instructions
/memory	Edit memory files
@file	Load specific files
@file in CLAUDE.md	Always include important files
@AGENTS.md	Reuse existing instructions

Key takeaway: Treat CLAUDE.md as a persistent system prompt for your project, and use @file references to give Claude exactly the context it needs. This leads to more accurate answers, better code changes, and less repeated explanation



The Big Picture

When modifying an existing project, Claude needs help in two areas:

Understanding what you want → Use screenshots.
Understanding how complex the task is → Use Planning Mode and Effort levels.

Think of it like this:

Problem	Tool
"Change this button/UI element"	Screenshot
"Understand the whole feature before coding"	Planning Mode
"Solve a difficult bug/logic problem"	Higher Effort
"Need both broad understanding and deep reasoning"	Planning + Higher Effort
1. Using Screenshots

Sometimes explaining a UI change with words is difficult.

Instead of saying:

Make the login button look better.

Show a screenshot and say:

Move this button below the form and make it green.

How to paste screenshots

Use:

Ctrl + V

Even on macOS, Claude Code uses:

Ctrl + V

for screenshots.

Why screenshots help

Claude can see:

Layout issues
Spacing problems
Visual bugs
Colors
Typography
Positioning

Example:

Before:

[ Username ]
[ Password ]

[ Login ]

You send a screenshot and ask:

Move Login to the right side and increase spacing.

Claude understands exactly which element you're talking about.

2. Planning Mode (/plan)

Planning Mode is for tasks that require understanding many files before coding.

Enable it with:

/plan

or

Shift + Tab twice
What happens in Planning Mode?

Instead of immediately editing files, Claude:

Step 1

Explores the codebase.

Reads:

Related files
Components
APIs
Configurations
Dependencies
Step 2

Creates a detailed plan.

Example:

1. Update User model
2. Add migration
3. Modify API endpoint
4. Update frontend form
5. Add validation
6. Add tests
Step 3

Shows you the plan.

Nothing is changed yet.

Step 4

Waits for approval.

You can review the approach before Claude touches code.

Why Planning Mode is useful

Without planning:

Prompt
   ↓
Code Changes

Claude may miss something.

With planning:

Prompt
   ↓
Research
   ↓
Plan
   ↓
Review
   ↓
Implementation

Much safer for large projects.

3. Editing the Plan

A very useful feature:

Ctrl + G

opens the generated plan in your editor.

You can modify it yourself.

Example:

Claude plan:

1. Update API
2. Update UI

You edit:

1. Update API
2. Update UI
3. Add audit logging
4. Add integration tests

Claude then follows the edited version.

This lets you steer implementation before any code is written.

4. Effort Levels (/effort)

Claude already reasons before responding.

Sometimes you need more thinking.

Check or change the level:

/effort
Available Levels
Low

Fastest.

Best for:

Simple edits
Small questions
Quick code generation

Example:

Rename this variable.
Medium (often default)

Balanced speed and quality.

Best for:

Typical development work
Feature additions
Standard debugging
High / Max

More reasoning.

Best for:

Difficult bugs
Architecture questions
Complex algorithms
Edge-case analysis

Example:

Why does this race condition occur only in production?

Higher effort helps Claude explore more possibilities.

5. The ultrathink Keyword

Sometimes you don't want to change the whole session's effort level.

For one specific prompt:

ultrathink:
Why does this distributed cache invalidation bug happen?

This tells Claude:

Spend extra effort on this response only.

Difference Between /effort and ultrathink
/effort

Changes session behavior.

Example:

/effort max

Now every prompt gets deeper reasoning.

ultrathink

Applies only to one prompt.

Example:

ultrathink
Find the root cause of this memory leak.

Next prompt returns to normal effort.

6. Planning Mode vs Effort

This is the most important distinction.

Many people confuse them.

Planning Mode = Breadth

Claude explores more of the codebase.

Questions like:

Which files are involved?
What components are connected?
What dependencies exist?

Example:

Add multi-factor authentication.

Requires understanding:

Backend
Frontend
Database
User flows

Use Planning Mode.

Higher Effort = Depth

Claude thinks harder about a specific problem.

Questions like:

Why does this algorithm fail?
What's causing this deadlock?
What's the optimal implementation?

Example:

Find the bug in this recursive parser.

Use higher effort.

7. Combining Both

For the hardest tasks, use both.

Example:

/plan
/effort max

Add a real-time collaborative editing system.

Claude will:

Explore many files
Build a plan
Think deeply about architecture
Wait for approval
Implement carefully

This gives both:

Broad understanding
Deep reasoning
Practical Examples
Example 1: Small UI Fix

Use:

Screenshot
Normal effort
Here's a screenshot.
Move the Save button below the form.

No planning needed.

Example 2: New Feature

Use:

Planning Mode
/plan

Add user profile pictures.

Claude researches first.

Example 3: Hard Bug

Use:

High effort
/effort max

Why does this memory leak happen?

Claude reasons more deeply.

Example 4: Large Complex Feature

Use:

Planning Mode
Max effort
/plan
/effort max

Implement role-based access control across the application.

Claude explores broadly and reasons deeply.

Mental Model

Think of the tools like this:

Screenshots = Better Communication

"This is what I mean."

Planning Mode = Bigger Map

"Understand the whole system before acting."

Effort Level = Stronger Thinking

"Analyze this more carefully."

Ultrathink = Temporary Boost

"Think extra hard about this one request."

Key Takeaway

Use screenshots when showing UI changes,
Planning Mode when Claude needs to understand many parts of your codebase,
and higher effort levels when the challenge is intellectually difficult. 
Planning improves breadth of understanding, while effort improves depth of reasoning. 
Combining both is ideal for large, complex engineering tasks.




This section teaches conversation management in Claude Code. The core idea is:

Don't treat the conversation as something you must passively accept. You can actively control, edit, rewind, summarize, and reset it to keep Claude focused.

Think of it like managing a junior developer: sometimes you stop them, redirect them, remove irrelevant discussion, or start a new task entirely.

The Big Picture

As conversations get longer, problems arise:

Claude may start solving the wrong problem.
It may focus on irrelevant details.
Old debugging discussions can clutter context.
Repeated mistakes can happen.

Claude provides tools to manage this:

Tool	Purpose
Escape	Stop Claude immediately
Escape + Memory	Fix recurring mistakes
Double Escape / /rewind	Go back in conversation history
/compact	Keep important knowledge, remove clutter
/clear	Start fresh
/resume	Return to an old conversation
1. Interrupting Claude with Escape

Sometimes Claude starts moving in the wrong direction.

Press:

Esc

This immediately stops generation.

Example

You ask:

Write tests for these 10 functions.

Claude starts:

I'll first analyze the entire architecture...
Then I'll build a comprehensive testing framework...
Then...

But you only want:

Test function #1 first.

Press:

Esc

Then say:

Focus only on function #1.

Now Claude stays focused.

Why This Is Powerful

Many people let the AI continue even when it's wrong.

Instead:

Wrong direction
      ↓
Press Esc
      ↓
Redirect immediately

This saves:

Time
Tokens
Frustration
2. Using Escape to Fix Repeated Mistakes

This is one of the most valuable workflows.

Suppose Claude repeatedly:

Uses the wrong coding pattern
Adds unnecessary comments
Writes tests incorrectly
Uses the wrong API
Workflow
Step 1

Stop Claude:

Esc
Step 2

Open memory:

/memory

or edit:

CLAUDE.md
Step 3

Add a rule.

Example:

Use Vitest, never Jest.

or

Do not create service classes.
Use functional modules.
Step 4

Continue.

Now future conversations inherit the correction.

Why This Matters

Bad workflow:

Conversation 1
"Use Vitest"

Conversation 2
"Use Vitest"

Conversation 3
"Use Vitest"

Good workflow:

One memory update
      ↓
Permanent correction
3. Rewinding Conversations

Long conversations accumulate junk.

Example:

Feature work
     ↓
Bug
     ↓
30 messages debugging
     ↓
Bug solved

Now those 30 messages are mostly irrelevant.

Rewind

Use:

Esc Esc

or:

/rewind

Claude shows conversation checkpoints.

You can jump back to an earlier state.

Example

Timeline:

Message 1: Build login feature
Message 2: Update API
Message 3: Debug deployment issue
Message 4: More deployment debugging
Message 5: More deployment debugging

Deployment issue is solved.

Rewind to:

Message 2

Continue from there.

Benefit

You keep:

✅ Project understanding

while removing:

❌ Distracting discussion

4. The /compact Command

One of the most important commands.

Use:

/compact
What It Does

Claude compresses:

Long conversation

into:

Short summary

while preserving important knowledge.

Example

Suppose Claude learned:

Project architecture
Database schema
API conventions
Authentication flow

after 100 messages.

You don't want to lose that knowledge.

But you also don't want all 100 messages.

Before
100 messages
After /compact
Summary of important findings

Claude remembers what matters.

Best Use Cases

When:

Context is valuable
Conversation is getting long
You're continuing related work
Mental Model
Conversation
     ↓
Extract knowledge
     ↓
Discard clutter
5. The /clear Command

Use:

/clear

when starting something completely different.

Example

Current task:

Fix React authentication

Next task:

Design Kubernetes deployment

These are unrelated.

Use:

/clear
Result

Claude starts with fresh context.

No assumptions from previous work.

Why This Helps

Without clearing:

Authentication context
        +
Deployment task

can confuse the model.

With clearing:

Fresh conversation

focused entirely on the new topic.

6. /resume

Clearing does not destroy history.

You can return later using:

/resume

Think of it as switching tabs.

Example:

Conversation A → Authentication
Conversation B → Infrastructure
Conversation C → Testing

Move between them when needed.

Compact vs Clear

This is the most important distinction.

/compact

Keeps knowledge.

Long conversation
      ↓
Summary
      ↓
Continue same project

Use when staying on related work.

/clear

Removes context.

Task A
      ↓
New conversation
      ↓
Task B

Use when switching topics.

Practical Workflow

Imagine you're building a feature.

Phase 1
Add user profiles

Claude learns:

User model
APIs
Database schema
Phase 2

Conversation gets long.

Run:

/compact

Keep the knowledge.

Phase 3

Claude starts over-engineering.

Press:

Esc

and redirect.

Phase 4

Claude repeatedly adds comments.

Open:

/memory

Add:

Use comments sparingly.
Phase 5

Finish user profiles.

Now switch to:

CI/CD pipeline redesign

Run:

/clear

Fresh start.

Mental Model

Think of these tools as conversation controls:

Escape

Stop. You're going the wrong way.

Memory Update

Remember this rule permanently.

Rewind

Go back to an earlier checkpoint.

Compact

Keep what matters, throw away clutter.

Clear

Start a completely new topic.

Resume

Return to an old thread.


Effective Claude users don't just write prompts—they actively manage context. 
Use Escape to redirect mistakes immediately, memory files to fix recurring issues permanently, 
rewind to remove unhelpful discussion, /compact to preserve useful knowledge while reducing clutter, 
and /clear when switching to unrelated work. These tools help keep Claude focused, accurate, and p
roductive during long development sessions.

