This lesson is about Hooks, one of the most powerful automation features in Claude Code.

If memory files teach Claude how to think, hooks control what happens before and after Claude acts.

The Big Idea

Normally, Claude works like this:

You ask something
       ↓
Claude decides to use a tool
       ↓
Tool runs
       ↓
Result returned to Claude
       ↓
Claude responds

Example:

"Fix this bug"
      ↓
Claude uses Edit tool
      ↓
File changes
      ↓
Claude responds

Hooks allow you to insert your own code into this process.

You ask something
       ↓
Claude wants to use tool
       ↓
HOOK
       ↓
Tool runs
       ↓
HOOK
       ↓
Claude continues

Think of hooks as:

"Automatic scripts that run around Claude's tool usage."

Why Hooks Matter

Without hooks:

Claude edits file
Done

With hooks:

Claude edits file
       ↓
Auto format
       ↓
Run tests
       ↓
Check linting
       ↓
Give feedback

The workflow becomes much more powerful.

Two Main Hook Types

Most people start with:

PreToolUse
PostToolUse
1. PreToolUse Hooks

Runs before Claude uses a tool.

Think:

"Claude wants to do something. Let me check first."

Example Flow
Claude wants to read secrets.txt
       ↓
PreToolUse hook runs
       ↓
Hook checks file
       ↓
Block access

Tool never executes.

What PreToolUse Can Do
Allow
Claude wants to read file
      ↓
Allowed
      ↓
Read executes
Block
Claude wants to read file
      ↓
Denied
      ↓
Error returned
Validate
Claude wants to edit file
      ↓
Check rules
      ↓
Allow or reject
Example Configuration
{
  "PreToolUse": [
    {
      "matcher": "Read",
      "hooks": [
        {
          "type": "command",
          "command": "node read_hook.js"
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}

Meaning:

Whenever Claude uses Read
      ↓
Run read_hook.js first
Real Example: Protect Secrets

Suppose your project contains:

.env.production

You never want Claude touching it.

PreToolUse:

Claude requests Read
      ↓
Hook checks filename
      ↓
If .env.production
      ↓
Reject

Result:

Access denied

Claude never sees the file.

2. PostToolUse Hooks

Runs after a tool finishes.

Think:

"Claude already did something. Now perform follow-up actions."

Example Flow
Claude edits file
      ↓
Edit completes
      ↓
PostToolUse hook runs
      ↓
Format code
Example Configuration
{
  "PostToolUse": [
    {
      "matcher": "Write|Edit",
      "hooks": [
        {
          "type": "command",
          "command": "node edit_hook.js"
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}

Meaning:

Whenever Write or Edit occurs
      ↓
Run edit_hook.js
Important Difference
PreToolUse

Can stop execution.

Hook
 ↓
Allow?
 ↓
Tool
PostToolUse

Cannot stop execution.

Tool
 ↓
Finished
 ↓
Hook

The action already happened.

Hook Matchers

Hooks target specific tools.

Example:

"matcher": "Read"

Only triggers on Read.

"matcher": "Write"

Only triggers on Write.

"matcher": "Edit"

Only triggers on Edit.

"matcher": "Write|Edit"

Triggers on both.

Think of matchers as filters.

Tool Type
     ↓
Matches?
     ↓
Run hook
Where Hooks Are Stored

Claude supports three locations.

Global Hooks
~/.claude/settings.json

Affects:

ALL projects

Example:

Always run prettier after edits.
Project Hooks
.claude/settings.json

Affects:

Entire team

Committed to Git.

Everyone gets the same behavior.

Local Project Hooks
.claude/settings.local.json

Affects:

Only you

Not committed.

Perfect for personal workflows.

Practical Use Cases

These are the most common real-world hook setups.

1. Auto Formatting

Problem:

Claude edits code.

Formatting may be inconsistent.

Solution:

PostToolUse
     ↓
Run prettier

Workflow:

Claude edits file
     ↓
Prettier runs
     ↓
Code formatted
2. Run Tests Automatically

Problem:

Claude changes code.

Nobody knows if tests pass.

Solution:

PostToolUse
     ↓
npm test

Workflow:

Claude edits file
     ↓
Tests run
     ↓
Results returned

Claude immediately sees failures.

3. Run Linting
Claude edits file
      ↓
ESLint
      ↓
Feedback returned

Claude can fix issues instantly.

4. Type Checking

For TypeScript:

Claude edits
      ↓
tsc
      ↓
Type errors returned

Claude gets immediate feedback.

5. Access Control

Prevent dangerous operations.

Example:

Block:
- Production configs
- Secrets
- Deployment files

Using PreToolUse.

6. Naming Convention Enforcement

Example rule:

React components must use PascalCase

Hook checks:

Button.tsx

Allowed.

button.tsx

Rejected.

7. Logging Claude Activity

Track:

Files read
Files edited
Commands executed

Useful in larger teams.

Mental Model

Think of hooks like Git hooks.

Git has:

pre-commit
post-commit

Claude has:

PreToolUse
PostToolUse

Same concept.

Complete Example

You ask:

Add a new login component.

Claude decides:

Edit Login.tsx

Flow:

Claude requests Edit
       ↓
PreToolUse Hook
       ↓
Validate file access
       ↓
Edit occurs
       ↓
PostToolUse Hook
       ↓
Run Prettier
       ↓
Run ESLint
       ↓
Run Tests
       ↓
Feedback returned

Everything happens automatically.

Key Takeaway

Hooks turn Claude from a simple AI assistant into a workflow-aware development agent.

PreToolUse hooks are gatekeepers: they can inspect, validate, or block actions before Claude performs them.
PostToolUse hooks are automators: they run follow-up tasks such as formatting, linting, testing, logging, or validation after Claude makes changes.

A useful rule of thumb:

PreToolUse = Control what Claude is allowed to do.
PostToolUse = Improve what Claude has already done.

That's why many advanced Claude Code setups use hooks to automatically enforce coding standards, run tests, protect sensitive files, and keep the development workflow largely hands-free.


